>> title: Sumashi-jiru
>> category: Soups
>> tags: japanese, dashi, tofu, soup, quick, light
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: すまし汁, sumashi-jiru, sumashi jiru, 澄まし汁, osumashi, o-sumashi, clear soup, clear dashi soup
>> pairs-with: dashi, gohan
>> servings: 4
>> time: 10 min
>> slack: narrow — usukuchi is saltier than the dark soy it looks paler than, and there is no thickness here to hide behind; past about a tablespoon in four cups the dashi is gone and only more dashi brings it back
>> step.1: The other half of 汁物, and the one a miso soup is not. There is nothing in it to cover a mistake, which is why it is the soup a household makes when the dashi is good.
>> step.2: bring to a bare simmer
>> step.3: warm through, no boil
>> step.4: scatter on top, off the heat

The other half of 汁物, and the one a miso soup is not. There is nothing in it to hide behind — three seasonings, and the dashi is the dish.

Bring @dashi{4%cups}(950 mL) seasoned with @usukuchi soy sauce{2%tsp}(10 mL; light-coloured and saltier than dark soy, not a substitute for it), @sake{1%Tbs}(15 mL) and @fine sea salt{1/2%tsp} to a bare simmer in a #saucepan{} ~warm{3%min}.

Slip @silken tofu{5%oz}(150 g; in 3/4-inch cubes) into @&(~1)seasoned dashi{} and hold it just under a simmer ~warm{2%min}, until the cubes are hot through and still whole. A boil breaks them.

Float @mitsuba{4%sprigs}(or 2 scallion greens, cut on a long bias) and @yuzu peel{1%piece}(a thumbnail, pared thin with no white) on @&(~1)soup{} in the bowl, off the heat, so the smell arrives before the spoon does.
